were it was deviled mutton,

00:43:39.315 --> 00:43:41.345
it was called, or something.

00:43:41.345 --> 00:43:42.317
- That sounds badass.

00:43:42.317 --> 00:43:45.329
- It was like the most
delicious marinated mutton

00:43:45.329 --> 00:43:46.464
I'd ever had in my life.

00:43:46.464 --> 00:43:47.297
- Damn.

00:43:47.297 --> 00:43:50.464
- But it was like doom level of spice.

00:43:51.384 --> 00:43:52.660
- Well, if it's got the word devil in it-

00:43:52.660 --> 00:43:54.344
- Devil, yeah.
- Devil's blood.

00:43:54.344 --> 00:43:55.721
- I'm tearing up, but I'm like,

00:43:55.721 --> 00:43:58.601
fuck, it's so good. (whimpering)

00:43:58.601 --> 00:43:59.712
Even though I was in so much pain,

00:43:59.712 --> 00:44:01.741
I'm like, just another bite.

00:44:01.741 --> 00:44:02.832
It's so good.

00:44:02.832 --> 00:44:04.188
- That's the thing about spicy food.

00:44:04.188 --> 00:44:05.783
If it's really good spicy food-

00:44:05.783 --> 00:44:06.616
- You power through, you power through.

00:44:06.616 --> 00:44:08.501
- Fuck me, just one more bite.

00:44:08.501 --> 00:44:09.868
When you take the bite, you're in a pain,

00:44:09.868 --> 00:44:12.807
but you're just like, oh,
it just tastes so good!

00:44:12.807 --> 00:44:13.640
- I don't know how to explain it,

00:44:13.640 --> 00:44:15.986
but when it's super spicy
and super delicious,

00:44:15.986 --> 00:44:18.048
it's almost like it's
searing in the flavor

00:44:18.048 --> 00:44:18.932
into your tongue.
- Right, right, right.

00:44:18.932 --> 00:44:21.952
- So I'm like, this is
so much flavor! (screams)

00:44:21.952 --> 00:44:24.088
- 'Cause it's almost like,
if you take the spice out,

00:44:24.088 --> 00:44:27.588
for example, I've had unspiced Thai curry.

00:44:28.881 --> 00:44:31.268
I've had unspiced Indian curry as well,

00:44:31.268 --> 00:44:33.024
'cause we live in England.
- No, don't do that.

00:44:33.024 --> 00:44:33.857
Come on now.

00:44:33.857 --> 00:44:34.854
- The best way I can describe it

00:44:34.854 --> 00:44:37.744
is that it just tastes flat.

00:44:37.744 --> 00:44:39.082
It tastes flat.

00:44:39.082 --> 00:44:41.766
There's something that's
missing from the taste.

00:44:41.766 --> 00:44:43.896
- It's like uncarbonated soda.

00:44:43.896 --> 00:44:45.043
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, kinda.

00:44:45.043 --> 00:44:46.802
That's a good analogy actually.

00:44:46.802 --> 00:44:49.634
The flavor's there, but there's a texture

00:44:49.634 --> 00:44:50.615
or something is missing.
- The flavor is there,

00:44:50.615 --> 00:44:51.965
but the personality is gone.

00:44:51.965 --> 00:44:53.672
- Exactly. (laughs)

00:44:53.672 --> 00:44:55.604
- Indian curry, if you
have rice with it too,

00:44:55.604 --> 00:44:56.437
or you have naan,

00:44:56.437 --> 00:44:58.813
I feel like that's so nice
to have spice with it,

00:44:58.813 --> 00:45:00.576
'cause then it kinda
helps mellow it out a bit.

00:45:00.576 --> 00:45:01.673
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, exactly.
- That's true, that's true.

00:45:01.673 --> 00:45:03.541
Yeah, see, 'cause I never,
for the longest time,

00:45:03.541 --> 00:45:06.688
I didn't understand that
concept of spice enhancing food,

00:45:06.688 --> 00:45:10.021
because up until, I'd say,
maybe three or four years ago,

00:45:10.021 --> 00:45:12.586
I could not handle spice at all.

00:45:12.586 --> 00:45:15.565
If I went to Coco Ichi,
I would do level one,

00:45:15.565 --> 00:45:16.886
which is like the most basic shit.

00:45:16.886 --> 00:45:19.747
And I'd just be like, "No,
too spicy, I can't do it."

00:45:19.747 --> 00:45:21.765
And then, I don't know what it was that,

00:45:21.765 --> 00:45:23.685
where just one day, I
kinda just flipped a dime.

00:45:23.685 --> 00:45:25.557
And I was like, you know what?

00:45:25.557 --> 00:45:29.066
It hurts, but I'm enjoying
it for some reason.

00:45:29.066 --> 00:45:30.149
What is this?

00:45:31.322 --> 00:45:35.024
- I had the opposite journey of Joey,

00:45:35.024 --> 00:45:40.024
someone who's grown up with
Thai food and stuff like that.

00:45:40.544 --> 00:45:41.393
I remember, when I was a kid,

00:45:41.393 --> 00:45:44.091
I really did not like
Japanese food at all,

00:45:44.091 --> 00:45:46.574
because a lot of Japanese cuisine revolves

00:45:46.574 --> 00:45:49.093
around the natural flavor of stuff.

00:45:49.093 --> 00:45:49.926
- [Joey] Savory.

00:45:49.926 --> 00:45:52.422
- Savoring it and just bringing
out the natural flavors

00:45:52.422 --> 00:45:53.342
of the ingredient.

00:45:53.342 --> 00:45:55.432
And me, a Thai person, being like,

00:45:55.432 --> 00:45:57.191
there is not enough
ingredients in this dish.

00:45:57.191 --> 00:45:58.211
What, sushi?

00:45:58.211 --> 00:45:59.381
Sushi, what, that's just-

00:45:59.381 --> 00:46:00.214
- [Joey] Two ingredients.

00:46:00.214 --> 00:46:01.256
- That's rice and fish.

00:46:01.256 --> 00:46:02.812
Where's the spice? Where's the flavor?

00:46:02.812 --> 00:46:03.645
- Wasabi.

00:46:04.609 --> 00:46:06.717
- But yeah, three ingredients like rice.

00:46:06.717 --> 00:46:10.080
You have rice with a bit of
vinegar in it, fish, and wasabi.

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:12.279
I'm just like, where's
the five million spices

00:46:12.279 --> 00:46:13.167
to go with it?

00:46:13.167 --> 00:46:15.254
What? You dip it in soy sauce?

00:46:15.254 --> 00:46:16.596
Where's the chilies in the soy sauce?

00:46:16.596 --> 00:46:17.429
- Where's the kick?

00:46:17.429 --> 00:46:19.613
- Yeah, where's the kick?

00:46:19.613 --> 00:46:21.684
Japanese cuisine was my
least favorite cuisine,

00:46:21.684 --> 00:46:25.736
because to me, there just
weren't enough extra spices

00:46:25.736 --> 00:46:28.125
or extra ingredients
to enhance the flavors.

00:46:28.125 --> 00:46:29.671
- Is this unseasoned sushi?

00:46:29.671 --> 00:46:31.671
(Garnt laughs)

00:46:31.671 --> 00:46:33.729
- And then, as I grew
older, I'm just like,

00:46:33.729 --> 00:46:37.110
as I grew older, I learned
to really appreciate

00:46:37.110 --> 00:46:41.193
the subtleties in the
flavor of Japanese cuisine.

00:46:42.759 --> 00:46:45.124
It really enhances the good ingredients.

00:46:45.124 --> 00:46:46.139
- It's just rice, isn't it?

00:46:46.139 --> 00:46:49.869
- It's just rice.
- It's all just the same.

00:46:49.869 --> 00:46:52.069
Sushi chef straight up for 20 years

00:46:52.069 --> 00:46:53.648
to get the perfect rice.

00:46:53.648 --> 00:46:54.567
It's all rice, isn't it?

00:46:54.567 --> 00:46:55.400
- It's all the same.

00:46:55.400 --> 00:46:56.498
- What is this, Uncle Ben's?

00:46:56.498 --> 00:46:57.331
What is this?

00:46:57.331 --> 00:46:58.469
(all laugh)

00:46:58.469 --> 00:47:00.189
- Did you put it through a colander?

00:47:00.189 --> 00:47:01.654
- Is this microwaved?

00:47:01.654 --> 00:47:02.728
- Did you wash this rice?

00:47:02.728 --> 00:47:05.379
- Did you wash this
rice through a colander?

00:47:05.379 --> 00:47:06.696
- It's microwave rice, right?

00:47:06.696 --> 00:47:08.758
Five-minute rice?

00:47:08.758 --> 00:47:09.707
- Minute Maid rice.

00:47:09.707 --> 00:47:11.208
- I fucking love the golden rice

00:47:11.208 --> 00:47:13.285
in those microwave packets though.

00:47:13.285 --> 00:47:14.646
I fucking love that rice.

00:47:14.646 --> 00:47:16.043
- Oh, the ready-to-make ones, right?

00:47:16.043 --> 00:47:16.876
- Yeah, dude.
- Yeah.

00:47:16.876 --> 00:47:18.010
- It tastes so good.

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:18.843
- [Joey] Yeah, they do taste good.

00:47:18.843 --> 00:47:20.130
- Probably 'cause it has all
the artificial shit in it.

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:21.152
- Yeah.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:47:21.152 --> 00:47:22.169
- Fast food rice.

00:47:22.169 --> 00:47:24.924
How do we make rice
unhealthy, super unhealthy?

00:47:24.924 --> 00:47:26.343
We found a way.

00:47:26.343 --> 00:47:30.145
- Yeah, honestly, the fast
food, the packeted fried rice

00:47:30.145 --> 00:47:31.933
that you can buy is actually pretty damn-

00:47:31.933 --> 00:47:33.054
- In Japan, it's pretty good.

00:47:33.054 --> 00:47:34.444
- The one that you buy in the freezer?

00:47:34.444 --> 00:47:36.079
- Yeah, yeah.
- I used to live off it.

00:47:36.079 --> 00:47:38.378
I was like, oh, well, I made
curry, and I don't wanna make,

00:47:38.378 --> 00:47:40.008
I didn't have a rice cooker at the time.

00:47:40.008 --> 00:47:42.808
And I hated making rice the
way that my mom taught me,

00:47:42.808 --> 00:47:46.357
which is just boil a pan
full of water and guess.

00:47:46.357 --> 00:47:48.928
Which sometimes works, sometimes didn't.

00:47:48.928 --> 00:47:51.077
- But doing it in Japan is blasphemous.

00:47:51.077 --> 00:47:52.708
- I know, I know, I know.

00:47:52.708 --> 00:47:54.565
It's weird, 'cause you'd
think that the first people

00:47:54.565 --> 00:47:58.298
to adapt an easier way to
do it would be white people,

00:47:58.298 --> 00:47:59.416
'cause they love being lazy.

00:47:59.416 --> 00:48:00.927
- [Joey] That's true.

00:48:00.927 --> 00:48:02.627
- You'd think rice cookers would go down,

00:48:02.627 --> 00:48:05.217
especially for the UK where we love rice.

00:48:05.217 --> 00:48:07.667
We love eating it with
curry, so you'd think that-

00:48:07.667 --> 00:48:09.320
- Do people in the UK love rice?

00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:10.461
- With curry.

00:48:10.461 --> 00:48:11.398
- That's pretty much it though.

00:48:11.398 --> 00:48:14.246
- But that's the only
thing we eat half the time.

00:48:14.246 --> 00:48:16.713
We eat chicken tikka masala,
it's like every week.

00:48:16.713 --> 00:48:17.546
- [Joey] That's true, that's true.

00:48:17.546 --> 00:48:18.841
- You'd think that we would figure out,

00:48:18.841 --> 00:48:20.530
hey, these rice cookers aren't so bad.

00:48:20.530 --> 00:48:21.363
- [Joey] Yeah.

00:48:21.363 --> 00:48:23.522
- 'Cause I remember growing up in the UK,

00:48:23.522 --> 00:48:26.453
whenever I'd have my
packed lunch of whatever,

00:48:26.453 --> 00:48:27.286
it would be like something-

00:48:27.286 --> 00:48:28.632
- No, you'd never make
rice for a packed lunch.

00:48:28.632 --> 00:48:29.663
It's a dinner thing.

00:48:29.663 --> 00:48:31.099
- Yeah, no, it's-
- No, sorry, tea, tea.

00:48:31.099 --> 00:48:32.442
No, fuck, it is dinner.
- Supper?

00:48:32.442 --> 00:48:34.080
- It is dinner.
- Supper.

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:35.349
- Whatever you wanna call it.
- Tea is a Northern thing.

00:48:35.349 --> 00:48:36.502
- Sorry, sorry.

00:48:36.502 --> 00:48:38.052
- Tea to me is the drink, you know?

00:48:38.052 --> 00:48:39.640
- I could see that, tea.

00:48:39.640 --> 00:48:40.983
I know, I know.

00:48:40.983 --> 00:48:42.200
- As with most of the-

00:48:42.200 --> 00:48:43.555
- I know, I know, I know.

00:48:43.555 --> 00:48:44.821
- As with most of the developed world.

00:48:44.821 --> 00:48:47.321
- Tea for me is a twit longer.

00:48:48.295 --> 00:48:49.164
- Awful joke.

00:48:49.164 --> 00:48:49.997
- Awful joke, I'm sorry.

00:48:49.997 --> 00:48:51.765
- No, but I remember
having this conversation

00:48:51.765 --> 00:48:53.326
with my mates, and they were like,

00:48:53.326 --> 00:48:57.428
"Wait, you eat rice every
day with every meal?"

00:48:57.428 --> 00:49:01.346
And I'm like, "Yeah, yeah,
you have it with every meal."

00:49:01.346 --> 00:49:03.375
They couldn't wrap their
heads around it, I guess.

00:49:03.375 --> 00:49:04.976
- You bougie motherfucker.

00:49:04.976 --> 00:49:07.095
- You eat rice with everything?

00:49:07.095 --> 00:49:10.175
You don't take a break
from rice? It's everything?

00:49:10.175 --> 00:49:12.902
I'm like, yeah, it's Asian cuisine.

00:49:12.902 --> 00:49:14.873
You literally eat rice with everything.

00:49:14.873 --> 00:49:16.104
- I'm sure the Asians are
asking the same thing,

00:49:16.104 --> 00:49:20.026
being like, wait, you use
bread with everything?

00:49:20.026 --> 00:49:21.276
Every meal is with bread?

00:49:21.276 --> 00:49:22.821
- It's bread or potatoes?

00:49:22.821 --> 00:49:23.654
Is that it?

00:49:23.654 --> 00:49:24.727
- Is that it?
- Bread and potatoes?

00:49:24.727 --> 00:49:25.759
- Why not both?

00:49:25.759 --> 00:49:27.004
(all laugh)

00:49:27.004 --> 00:49:28.415
- Carbs on carbs.

00:49:28.415 --> 00:49:30.680
- I mean, I think it's grown
a lot in popularity though

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:32.026
in the last 10 years especially.

00:49:32.026 --> 00:49:32.859
- Rice?

00:49:32.859 --> 00:49:35.278
- Yeah, definitely not when
I was growing up at all.

00:49:35.278 --> 00:49:36.721
- It's become way more popular,

00:49:36.721 --> 00:49:39.138
rice-based dishes in general.

00:49:40.250 --> 00:49:41.801
- I say finally.

00:49:41.801 --> 00:49:42.750
What took them so long?
- Right, right, right.

00:49:42.750 --> 00:49:43.583
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:49:43.583 --> 00:49:44.993
- But I think there's enough dishes

00:49:44.993 --> 00:49:47.622
where people are making
them where a rice cooker...

00:49:47.622 --> 00:49:48.521
People buy bread makers.

00:49:48.521 --> 00:49:50.483
People aren't making
bread every fucking week.

00:49:50.483 --> 00:49:51.858
And those things are fucking massive.

00:49:51.858 --> 00:49:52.888
- [Joey] Yeah, right.

00:49:52.888 --> 00:49:54.326
- My little rice cooker is this big.

00:49:54.326 --> 00:49:56.958
But the point was, before I did this,

00:49:56.958 --> 00:49:58.905
I used to just buy the microwave packets.

00:49:58.905 --> 00:49:59.765
They're amazing.

00:49:59.765 --> 00:50:04.085
300-gram packets, perfect
portion, tastes great.

00:50:04.085 --> 00:50:06.673
Slap it on the plate, you're good to go.

00:50:06.673 --> 00:50:08.845
Amazing, amazing.

00:50:08.845 --> 00:50:11.215
- I actually think those,

00:50:11.215 --> 00:50:13.410
I also survived without a rice cooker

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:14.806
in university for a long time,

00:50:14.806 --> 00:50:17.225
'cause the microwave
packets were good enough.

00:50:17.225 --> 00:50:20.223
The problem with a rice cooker,

00:50:20.223 --> 00:50:23.386
I find, is that the rice
goes off so fucking quick.

00:50:23.386 --> 00:50:25.437
So either you cook a tiny bit of rice

00:50:25.437 --> 00:50:27.127
in this massive rice cooker,

00:50:27.127 --> 00:50:29.934
because I'm Asian, and of course,

00:50:29.934 --> 00:50:31.608
if a mom buys you a rice cooker,

00:50:31.608 --> 00:50:33.446
it's like the biggest fucking rice cooker

00:50:33.446 --> 00:50:34.387
you can ever imagine.

00:50:34.387 --> 00:50:36.417
- How many families do you wanna feed?

00:50:36.417 --> 00:50:39.909
- It's like those restaurants
ones where it's this big.

00:50:39.909 --> 00:50:41.576
- Luckily, there was
a humanitarian crisis,

00:50:41.576 --> 00:50:42.445
but it wasn't for long,

00:50:42.445 --> 00:50:45.626
'cause Garnt had his rice
cooker that his mom gave him.

00:50:45.626 --> 00:50:47.825
- Yeah, 'cause my mom bought me

00:50:47.825 --> 00:50:49.526
a rice cooker for university,

00:50:49.526 --> 00:50:52.609
'cause that's one of the
essential pieces of equipment

00:50:52.609 --> 00:50:53.697
that you need for university

00:50:53.697 --> 00:50:55.366
for every Asian kid
that their mom provides.

00:50:55.366 --> 00:50:56.199
- [Joey] Of course.

00:50:56.199 --> 00:50:57.565
- They provide you a rice cooker

00:50:57.565 --> 00:51:00.565
that could survive a nuclear winter.

00:51:02.883 --> 00:51:04.082
- I'm pretty sure you
could hop inside of it

00:51:04.082 --> 00:51:05.214
and survive a blast, right?

00:51:05.214 --> 00:51:06.743
- Yeah, right, right.

00:51:06.743 --> 00:51:09.843
But the problem is, I would
always cook too much rice

00:51:09.843 --> 00:51:12.254
or too little rice, right?

00:51:12.254 --> 00:51:14.666
It would be either too much
for the meal I was cooking,

00:51:14.666 --> 00:51:16.909
and I would have some leftover rice.

00:51:16.909 --> 00:51:18.011
Or I would cook too much rice,

00:51:18.011 --> 00:51:20.043
and the rice just goes off so quickly,

00:51:20.043 --> 00:51:22.333
even if you put in the
fridge or something.

00:51:22.333 --> 00:51:23.832
Because unless you're really,

00:51:23.832 --> 00:51:25.563
and this is the Asian trap, right?

00:51:25.563 --> 00:51:26.602
Because with the rice cooker,

00:51:26.602 --> 00:51:29.494
you have to eat rice every day

00:51:29.494 --> 00:51:30.507
in order to eat all the rice

00:51:30.507 --> 00:51:31.755
that you cooked with the rice cooker.

00:51:31.755 --> 00:51:33.209
Otherwise, it's just gonna go off.

00:51:33.209 --> 00:51:34.196
- [Joey] Exactly.

00:51:34.196 --> 00:51:36.054
- So that's why actually
the microwave packets

00:51:36.054 --> 00:51:40.031
were just the perfect
portion for one meal,

00:51:40.031 --> 00:51:41.441
and it was just a lot less effort.

00:51:41.441 --> 00:51:44.130
- Yeah, because then you
have to start experimenting.

00:51:44.130 --> 00:51:46.017
You look in your fridge
and be like, all right,

00:51:46.017 --> 00:51:48.705
what can the rice go with
that I haven't tried yet?

00:51:48.705 --> 00:51:53.147
- However can you dehydrate
the rice that you've cooked,

00:51:53.147 --> 00:51:54.578
turn it back into a rice grain?

00:51:54.578 --> 00:51:55.556
Is that possible?

00:51:55.556 --> 00:51:56.389
- Well, I mean-

00:51:56.389 --> 00:51:57.653
- Could you do that if
you had the technology?

00:51:57.653 --> 00:51:59.025
I'm asking is it possible.

00:51:59.025 --> 00:52:00.115
- Well, I mean, I've put a-

00:52:00.115 --> 00:52:00.948
- Have you tried?

00:52:00.948 --> 00:52:02.185
- [Ashley] No, it's like popcorn.

00:52:02.185 --> 00:52:04.284
You don't turn back the popcorn to its-

00:52:04.284 --> 00:52:06.542
- Oh, that form is
completely different though.

00:52:06.542 --> 00:52:07.937
The rice stays the same.

00:52:07.937 --> 00:52:09.656
I think if you took the water out somehow,

00:52:09.656 --> 00:52:10.503
maybe we could do it.

00:52:10.503 --> 00:52:13.462
Scientists, can we un-hydrate rice?

00:52:13.462 --> 00:52:15.943
- My family, or actually a
lot of Japanese families,

00:52:15.943 --> 00:52:17.725
what they do is, if
they make too much rice,

00:52:17.725 --> 00:52:20.913
then you put it into small
maybe microwave-sized packets,

00:52:20.913 --> 00:52:22.494
and then you put it in the freezer.

00:52:22.494 --> 00:52:25.703
- Yes, that was also
what happened as well.

00:52:25.703 --> 00:52:27.219
- But it's not the same.

00:52:27.219 --> 00:52:29.275
The next morning, you wake up being like,

00:52:29.275 --> 00:52:30.961
